Struct return + chained method calls
====================================

A function that returns a struct by value forces the
caller to materialize a return slot. The codegen then has
to either:

  - Pass a hidden pointer (sret-style) to the callee, or
  - Return the struct in registers / a typed return slot.

When the result is immediately read (`make_pair().first`)
or chained into another struct-returning call, the
temporary lifetime has to be correct or the read goes off
into freed / clobbered memory.

This test covers:

  - returning a small struct and reading a field directly,
  - chaining two struct-returning calls,
  - storing a returned struct in a local and reading it.

    module sysl.tests.lang_features

    struct Pair
        first: int
        second: int

    make_pair(a: int, b: int) -> Pair = Pair(a, b)

    add_to(p: Pair, n: int) -> Pair = Pair(p.first + n, p.second + n)

    #test
    test_direct_field_read() -> unit
        assert(make_pair(3, 4).first == 3, "first")
        assert(make_pair(3, 4).second == 4, "second")

    #test
    test_struct_in_local() -> unit
        val p = make_pair(10, 20)
        assert(p.first == 10, "local.first")
        assert(p.second == 20, "local.second")

    #test
    test_chained_struct_return() -> unit
        val p = add_to(make_pair(1, 2), 100)
        assert(p.first == 101,  s"chained first expected 101, got ${p.first}")
        assert(p.second == 102, s"chained second expected 102, got ${p.second}")
